    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 71 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\n**The RFC7807 requirement (the stated spec) was already satisfied** by the existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice`: every rejection path (400/409/429) throws a typed exception whose handler returns a Spring `ProblemDetail`, which serializes as `application/problem+json` with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. The new acceptance test `Cp59Tests` (asserting the `application/problem+json` content type and a `status` member) passes as-is, and all rejection handlers route through the same `ProblemDetail` mechanism, so no code change was needed there.\n\n**What was actually blocking a green suite** were two *unrelated*, pre-existing failures \u2014 `Cp23` (household-points) and `Cp39` (tenure-cap) \u2014 both failing on `membershipLevel` (expected 3, got 2). Root cause: `cappedMembershipLevel(...)` enforces the \"level \u2264 one above the household's current max\" ceiling (the `Cp51` checkpoint) using each household member's **stored** level. That stored value is a stale snapshot from when the member was created and the household was still too small to earn the +2 household-of-3 points. So a third joiner who legitimately reached 5 points / level 3 was pinned to 2 by members whose snapshots hadn't caught up.\n\n**Fix** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`): the ceiling now evaluates each existing household member at the household's *current* size (`max(storedLevel, levelRecomputedAtCurrentSize)`). When a household grows large enough to qualify for the household points, every member's standing rises with it, lifting the ceiling for later joiners \u2014 while `Cp51`'s `lb \u2264 la + 1` invariant still holds (verified: 2 \u2264 2).\n\nResult: full suite **71/71 green**, including `Cp59` (problem+json), `Cp23`, `Cp39`, and the `Cp51` ceiling test.",
